<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip III of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1245–1285</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Philip the Bold</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip III, called the Bold, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1270 to 1285, the son of Louis IX. He inherited the lands of the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, and in 1274 he handed the Comtat Venaissin, which had passed to the French crown after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-x">Pope Gregory X</a>. He died in 1285 after a failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against Aragon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-iii-of-france.jpg" alt="Philip III of France" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philip_III_of_France.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
